{"parentUuid":"ea3b8f97-7692-40de-ad38-f489b3ad7558","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"90b31a4a-02d6-45cd-8de8-de66881e6da3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0161n54wAGGVQEmGEvn9qeuW","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: aa6055acd1b6e26f5 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'aa6055acd1b6e26f5', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/aa6055acd1b6e26f5.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"b35ad9ef-e993-49ed-a226-fbcc585ced63","timestamp":"2026-11-23T19:19:07.914Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"aa6055acd1b6e26f5","description":"Implement VR-2 slice 1","resolvedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","prompt":"Implement slice 1 of unit VR-2 for the Nexus Code CLI, exactly as planned in `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (read the whole plan first; sections 1, 3, 4, 5, 6 and the slice-1 entry in section 8 are your spec; section \"Decisions taken\" is binding). Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2` (branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`). Do not commit, push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open, choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nSlice 1 in short: a shared verification service that runs the existing verify core (`verifyChange` in `cli/src/core/verify.ts`; no second verification path, no change to verify.ts or the v3 result schema) in a forked worker process, and an MCP tool `nexus_verify` on `nexus serve-mcp` that an UNTRUSTED caller can invoke with NO arguments to get a verdict and a receipt id, plus `resources/read nexus://verify-receipt/<id>` to read the receipt back.\n\nSecurity properties you must implement and test (the caller is untrusted):\n- The tool takes no arguments; any argument at all is rejected with an error and nothing runs.\n- Only the operator's verifier runs (the flags given when `serve-mcp` was started, as the plan specifies); a verifier discovered in the repo is never approved here: the result is the existing BLOCKED / VERIFIER_NOT_APPROVED style outcome with nothing executed. The caller is never prompted.\n- The base commit is pinned at server start; what is checked is the working tree on disk in the served workspace.\n- Sandbox on, network off, no opt-out. Refuse in read-only mode and where managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`) requires a human for command execution, with a clear error.\n- One verification at a time per process, not queued; timeout and cancellation enforced by killing the worker's process group by pid (never `pkill`); output size caps.\n- Receipts: id `vr-` + 32 random hex, in memory only (cap 32, oldest evicted), SHA-256 of the served document included; a caller cannot create, overwrite or forge one. Every verdict (including BLOCKED) is a successful tool result with a receipt; an error only when no verdict exists.\n\nFiles (per the plan): new `cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`, `cli/src/core/verify_worker.ts`; edit `cli/src/mcp.ts`; in `cli/src/index.ts` edit ONLY the `serve-mcp` command (around line 1194; another agent holds a claim on the rest of the file — do not touch the `acp` command or anything else); one exported helper in `cli/src/config.ts` if the plan calls for it; docs `docs/MCP_GUIDE.md` and `docs/USER_MANUAL.md` (short, factual sections; state the same-user tampering limit from the plan's Risks honestly). New tests `cli/test/verify_service.test.ts` and `cli/test/mcp_verify.test.ts` covering section 6 of the plan for the MCP side: a scripted MCP client gets a verdict and a receipt id and reads the receipt; hostile requests (any argument: a verifier command, a path, a claimed outcome, extra fields; a forged or unknown receipt id; oversized input; two concurrent calls) cannot raise the verdict; a seeded failing verifier is not VERIFIED; a weakened-test diff is not VERIFIED (reuse the VR-1 fixtures/helpers under `cli/test/helpers/`, see how `cli/test/verify_hostile.test.ts` and `cli/test/verify_command.test.ts` build repos); a discovered-but-unapproved verifier runs nothing; timeout kills the worker and its children.\n\nFirst thing to settle (the plan flags it as unknown): how to fork the worker so it works both from source under the test runner (`node --import tsx`) on Node 22 and 24 and from the built `dist/` (plain `.js`). Look at how the repo already spawns its own TypeScript entry points in both modes (e.g. the sidecar, `cli/test/helpers/audit_fixture.ts` `cliEntry()`, any existing `fork`/`spawn(process.execPath` uses in `cli/src`) and follow that pattern. Only Node 24 is installed here; write the code so it does not depend on Node-24-only behaviour.\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Every test or probe: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/vr2/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference, no network.\n- Type-check with `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` (must be clean). `cli/node_modules` is a symlink and the Rust binaries are already in `core/target/release/`; do not run `npm install` or cargo.\n- New test files must import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first (the repo's leak guard fails a file that leaves timers, child processes or servers behind): clear every timer and stop every child you start, by its own pid.\n- Stress the new async tests: run each new test file 5 times in a row and 4 copies concurrently once; fix any flake (tight deadlines are the usual cause).\n- Also run, to make sure nothing regressed: `test/mcp*.test.ts` (whatever exists for the MCP server), `test/verify_command.test.ts`, `test/verify_hostile.test.ts`, `test/verify_sandbox.test.ts`, `test/check_requirements.test.ts`, and `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs` and `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs` from the repo root. If a repo gate requires a ledger/doc entry for a new tool or flag, add it.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly, in plain sentences. Minimum code that meets the spec; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n- If you find that a planned decision cannot work as written, do the closest conservative thing, and say so in the report; do not silently drop a security property or a test.\n\nReport (under 500 words): files added/changed with line counts; how the worker is launched in source and dist modes; each security property and the test that proves it; exact test results (file: pass/fail counts, the stress results); gates run; deviations from the plan and why; anything not done.","outputFile":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/aa6055acd1b6e26f5.output","canReadOutputFile":true,"canContinueAgent":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ea3b8f97-7692-40de-ad38-f489b3ad7558","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"s