{"parentUuid":"549fb945-cf65-4b20-b8c2-d32b241c9a3c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01EFxJHfMX3mjLfTQEXRPJ14","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"claude-mem hook claude-code file-context","durationMs":492},"type":"attachment","uuid":"70858812-feff-4978-a55d-32194373e5f8","timestamp":"2026-11-23T20:42:15.177Z","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"549fb945-cf65-4b20-b8c2-d32b241c9a3c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7ea09646-7265-455a-a4f2-2432417f5c93","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFxJHfMX3mjLfTQEXRPJ14","type":"tool_result","content":"<<<<<<< HEAD\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol. No check inside the server stops any of the following:\n\n- It can change what verification links in from the workspace: installed dependencies (`node_modules`, `.venv`), which Git ignores.\n- It can edit the server user's home: `~/.config/nexus/config.env` (operator settings such as `NEXUS_VERIFIER_ENV_PASSTHROUGH`), a user-tier managed policy, and the user's Git configuration.\n- It can replace the operator's verifier script, or a binary the verifier finds on `PATH`, when these live outside the workspace: they are as writable by that user as anything else. The Nexus installation itself is too.\n- A receipt is not secret from the client. It carries the workspace path, changed file names and the end of the output of a verifier run that did not pass, as the `nexus verify` result document does.\n- If the server is killed with SIGKILL, the running verifier goes on until its own deadline and the scratch directories of that verification stay in the temp directory. SIGTERM and SIGINT stop the verification and remove them before the server exits.\n\nWhere the client is not trusted to that degree, run the server as a separate user or in CI.\n||||||| 4196a306\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol: change the installed dependencies that verification links in (`node_modules`, `.venv`, which Git ignores), the user's Git configuration, or the Nexus installation itself. No check inside the server stops that.\n=======\nThe ACP server exposes the same service as `_nexus/verify`. It accepts only an active `sessionId`; the verifier and deliverables are fixed by the operator configuration, and the base commit is pinned when the session starts or loads. Verification is refused while an overlapping session is prompting or stopping. Read the result with `_nexus/receipt/get` using the returned receipt id and the same session id. See [ACP.m\n\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol: change the installed dependencies that verification links in (`node_modules`, `.venv`, which Git ignores), the user's Git configuration, or the Nexus installation itself. No check inside the server stops that.\n>>>>>>> origin/v1/vr2-verify-for-clients\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"075e39a0-a5a0-4165-a838-46fa182d4148","timestamp":"2026-11-23T20:42:15.425Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"<<<<<<< HEAD\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol. No check inside the server stops any of the following:\n\n- It can change what verification links in from the workspace: installed dependencies (`node_modules`, `.venv`), which Git ignores.\n- It can edit the server user's home: `~/.config/nexus/config.env` (operator settings such as `NEXUS_VERIFIER_ENV_PASSTHROUGH`), a user-tier managed policy, and the user's Git configuration.\n- It can replace the operator's verifier script, or a binary the verifier finds on `PATH`, when these live outside the workspace: they are as writable by that user as anything else. The Nexus installation itself is too.\n- A receipt is not secret from the client. It carries the workspace path, changed file names and the end of the output of a verifier run that did not pass, as the `nexus verify` result document does.\n- If the server is killed with SIGKILL, the running verifier goes on until its own deadline and the scratch directories of that verification stay in the temp directory. SIGTERM and SIGINT stop the verification and remove them before the server exits.\n\nWhere the client is not trusted to that degree, run the server as a separate user or in CI.\n||||||| 4196a306\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol: change the installed dependencies that verification links in (`node_modules`, `.venv`, which Git ignores), the user's Git configuration, or the Nexus installation itself. No check inside the server stops that.\n=======\nThe ACP server exposes the same service as `_nexus/verify`. It accepts only an active `sessionId`; the verifier and deliverables are fixed by the operator configuration, and the base commit is pinned when the session starts or loads. Verification is refused while an overlapping session is prompting or stopping. Read the result with `_nexus/receipt/get` using the returned receipt id and the same session id. See [ACP.m\n\n**What this does not protect against.** The guarantee is about the request. `serve-mcp` gives the same client `run_command`, and a client that can run commands as the server's user can act outside the protocol: change the installed dependencies that verification links in (`node_modules`, `.venv`, which Git ignores), the user's Git configuration, or the Nexus installation itself. No check inside the server stops that.\n>>>>>>> origin/v1/vr2-verify-for-clients","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/mike/nexus-code","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sandboxed":false,"so