{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"f0a8b04a528152dc634c774f755a6eecfcd56443160c749e5669a7a64d517fb4","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Both open PRs got new commits (#758 and #760), so I'm sending each for a re-review against its earlier findings."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SJWxsm12VWueZxCaTv3gW7","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #758 new head","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Re-review GitHub PR #758 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 758 --comments`, `gh pr diff 758`), \"fix(goal): pause stale boot goals with visible reason (PA-3 …)\", base `release/v1.3.0`. The previous review (the long comment on the PR beginning \"Independent review at `8d5b5465…`\") requested changes. The author pushed a new head (short `725a4435`; take the full SHA from `gh pr view 758 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved). Look at `git diff 7320b78a..<new> -- cli docs` for what changed.\n\nVerify, by running:\n1. BLOCKER 1: a process restart (not only a host reboot) must leave a previously active goal PAUSED with a visible reason. Previously the \"boot id\" was `/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id`, so a crash or clean exit followed by a restart on the same boot left the goal RUNNING; a state with no `activeBootId` (written before the PR) also stayed RUNNING; on hosts without `/proc` nothing paused. Check the new mechanism: what identifies \"this process\", how liveness of the previous owner is decided (pid reuse? a lock? a nonce?), and that these cases come back PAUSED: state RUNNING / WAITING / BLOCKED written by another (dead) process identity; state with no identity field at all; and that a goal owned by a process that is STILL ALIVE (a second concurrent `nexus` on the same state) is not wrongly paused or stolen. Already PAUSED / COMPLETE / CANCELLED goals must be left as they were. Drive the real functions with seeded state files in a throwaway HOME (see how `cli/test/goal_lifecycle.test.ts` builds state), not only by reading.\n2. BLOCKER 2: the test must exercise it. Remove the stale-owner checks (wherever they now live) and confirm `cli/test/goal_lifecycle.test.ts` fails; restore with `git checkout`. Confirm the tests seed non-paused states with a foreign identity and with none, and no longer return early / pass vacuously on hosts without `/proc`.\n3. Earlier non-blocking items, status only: `pauseReason ?? stale` hiding the stale reason behind an older operator reason; the doc `docs/v1.3.0/PA3_GOAL_LIFECYCLE.md` stating exactly what is covered; `delete (state as any)…` casts.\n4. The delta must not let model text, a tool call or workspace content change a goal's lifecycle state, and must not loosen approval, budget or managed-policy checks (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`); does resuming a paused goal go through the same checks as starting one?\n5. The token-usage commit `7320b78a` (rolled-up sub-operation token usage in `cli/src/goal.ts`, `goal_command.ts`): any way it misreports, double counts, or exposes data it should not? Light check.\n6. Run at head, one file at a time: every `cli/test/goal*.test.ts` and `cli/test/operation*.test.ts`; for any failure check the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev758b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev758b/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/rev758b/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev758b`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one file at a time, timeout at most 9 minutes (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). Probe scripts live in a separate scratch directory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops. Aim for about 30 minutes.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 450 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; each blocker fixed / partly / not with file:line and what you ran; status of the non-blocking items; new findings ranked, blocking vs not; mutation result; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KnA2jbquo4wFMygedyPLgG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #760 new head","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Re-review GitHub PR #760 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 760 --comments`, `gh pr diff 760`), \"feat(budget): bound objective inference submissions\", base `release/v1.3.0`. The previous review (the comment on the PR beginning \"Independent review at `75a838be…`\") requested changes. The author pushed a new head (short `f4abea7a`; take the full SHA from `gh pr view 760 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved). Look at `git diff 75a838be..<new>` for what changed.\n\nVerify, by running:\n1. BLOCKER: `cli/test/protocol_parity.test.ts` failed (\"checked-in schema samples equal a fresh generation\") because `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` was not regenerated after `sidecar-v1.schema.json` changed. Run that test at the new head; run `node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs` (or its check mode) in the throwaway worktree and confirm `git status` shows no diff afterwards (then `git checkout` anything it touched). Check whether a Rust-side parity test reads the same fixture (search `core/` for the fixture name) and, if the Rust sources that consume it changed or need to, say so; build/test Rust only if cheap with an isolated `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` — otherwise report it as not run.\n2. CI at the previous head was red on `test-shard (22, 1)`, `(22, 2)`, `(24, 1)`, `(24, 2)`. Find out which tests failed there (`gh run list --branch v1/p10-02-inference-submission-budget --workflow ci.yml --limit 3`, then `gh run view <id> --log-failed | grep -E \"✖|not ok\" | sort -u | head -40` for the completed run at 75a838be) and whether each is fixed at the new head by running that test file locally at the new head. If a failing test is unrelated to this PR, show it also fails on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n3. Earlier non-blocking items, status: docs now say that findings extraction and the two compaction calls (`agent.ts` ~3307, ~3758, ~3797) are not counted (or they are counted now); docs say a project `.env` can set `NEXUS_MAX_INFERENCE_SUBMISSIONS`; docs say the counter is per turn without durable state; the P10 plan progress note says delegated paths remain outside the bound.\n4. The delta must not weaken what held before: at the limit the turn ends `LIMIT_REACHED` / `INFERENCE_SUBMISSION_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED` with nothing retried; the reservation is saved before dispatch; resume can only lower the ceiling; invalid config throws. Re-run `cli/test/p10_inference_submission_budget.test.ts` and one mutation (disable the admission check → the file must fail; restore with `git checkout`).\n5. Run at head, one file at a time: `p10_inference_submission_budget`, `protocol_parity`, `protocol_schema`, `run_json_contract`, `context_convergence`, `sidecar`, `check_requirements`. For any failure check the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev760b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev760b/c